  "worked_solution": "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x)))}^{5}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x)))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x))=4-3(2)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x))=6-2=4\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x)))}^{5}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x)))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32}{64} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
